<?php
/** User manual — Project Manager edition: how a Golden A/S project manager runs a project in the portal, day by day.   GAS-CP-R29-011 */
return [
  'title' => 'Customer Portal — Project Manager Handbook',
  'lede'  => 'The working routine of a Golden A/S project manager in the portal: setting a project up, the daily round, the approvals you own, the weekly cycle, the project meeting, the monthly closing and the close-out — with checklists.',
  'sections' => [
    ['id' => 'role', 'title' => 'What the portal expects from the project manager', 'body' => [
      'The project manager (PM) named on a project is the owner of everything that happens in it: the portal sends you every notification of the project, lists the project in your attention list, pre-selects you as approver and addresses the report reminders to you. The owner\'s side sees the project through what you keep up to date — the stages, the weekly notes, the reports, the approvals and the open issues — so the quality of the portal for the customer is the quality of your routine.',
      ['fig' => 'roles.svg', 'cap' => 'What each kind of account sees and does — the PM works on the Golden A/S side, the owner sees only external items'],
      ['ul' => ['You own four things for each sub-project: the progress (stages, deadline, weekly notes), the documents (drawings, owner approvals, class submittals, tests, files), the work (tickets, work orders, service and inspection reports, planning) and the register of what is still open (open issues).', 'You approve or move on: owner approvals you submit, work orders you request or approve, service reports you push to acceptance, timesheets you approve, offers you follow up.', 'Everything internal stays internal: an internal note, comment or file is never shown to the owner. When in doubt, mark it internal and decide later.', 'The reports are generated from the data — they are only as good as the entries behind them. A weekly report with "Nothing to report" is a message to the owner too.']],
      ['tip' => 'Keep the rule simple: if it happened on the project and is not in the portal, it did not happen. Phone calls and e-mails get a weekly note, an internal note on the offer, a ticket comment or an issue.'],
      'Finding things in this handbook and the other manuals: the search box at the top of Library › Support searches every section of the three editions at once and opens the manual at the matching section; the filter box above the contents of each manual does the same while you type. The What\'s new section at the end of every manual lists the latest portal releases.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'setup', 'title' => 'Setting a project up', 'body' => [
      'A main project is the ERP project code (GY-85-02); the sub-projects are its Y.nn parts. Load the structure from SoftOne (Settings › Import from Excel › Project structures) or create it from Fleet › Projects › + Project / + Sub-project. Then walk through the checklist below once — ten minutes that save hours every week.',
      ['fig' => 'g-projects.jpg', 'cap' => 'The project tree — projects, sub-projects, offers and the roll-ups'],
      ['ol' => ['Project: number given by the portal (PRJ.000496 — or type the ERP code), vessel, customer, branch, project manager (you), status Active, the contacts of the project (owner\'s representative, yard, class surveyor) on the Contacts tab; on each sub-project (Y.001 automatic) tick the contacts in relation with it on its Contacts tab — the report e-mails go to them first — and pick the invoicing identity of the vessel it is billed to.', 'Sub-projects: code and name as in the ERP, system, deadline, invoicing status; tick N/A under the stages that do not exist for this part (a pure supply has no INST / SAT).', 'Team: on the Process tab of every sub-project tick the engineers who will work on it — they then find it in their timesheet, service report and inspection report forms.', 'Drawings register: one line per drawing expected from the yard, owner, maker or Golden A/S, with the date sent and the date expected.', 'Owner approvals and class submittals: list the documents that will need approval with their numbers, even before the first revision exists — the pending list becomes your agenda.', 'Equipment: register the units of the vessel (shore converters, Marex, Aventics) with their serial numbers, or import them from the maker lists; service records then accumulate by themselves.', 'Report reminders: Work › Tasks › Report reminders creates the weekly, monthly, timesheet and progress-update reminders for the project in one go.', 'Planning: put the first mobilisation on the Planning board so the engineers see it, the cars are reserved and the open items travel with them.', 'Decks & working areas: upload the deck plans on the vessel page and draw the working areas once (Interior, Exterior, Machinery, Technical, Guest, Crew) — the weekly works forecast of every project picks from them.', 'Remote support: if the owner has a remote support contract, register it under Library › IT & Cybersecurity (period, hours, response time, access) — the vessel\'s tickets are then covered by it and the renewal reminder is created for you.', 'One vessel record: before creating anything, check that the vessel exists once (Settings › Vessel duplicates lists the suspects); a second record of the same hull is refused by the vessel form.']],
      ['fig' => 'stages.svg', 'cap' => 'The seven stages — ENG · PROC · PROD · FAT · INST · SAT/HAT · COMM'],
      ['fig' => 'g-team.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Team box of a sub-project — tick who works on it'],
      ['tip' => 'Name the sub-project contacts (owner\'s electrical engineer, yard project manager) on the project Contacts tab and link them to the equipment they are responsible for: the service reports, the weekly report distribution and the equipment documents pick them up.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'daily', 'title' => 'The daily round — 15 minutes in the morning', 'body' => [
      'Open the dashboard first. The attention list and the bell contain everything that changed since you last looked; the order below is the order of urgency.',
      ['fig' => 'g-dashboard.jpg', 'cap' => 'The staff dashboard — attention list, My day, My sub-projects, My week'],
      ['ol' => ['Notifications (bell): approvals decided, reports disputed, tickets answered by the owner, offer notes, tasks due. Open each one from the bell; it is marked seen when opened.', 'Tickets: new and unassigned tickets of your projects — assign an engineer or yourself, set In progress, place the ticket under the right project / sub-project. A ticket the owner answered needs a reply the same day; one waiting on the owner for more than a week needs a nudge.', 'Service reports submitted: every submitted report is waiting for the owner\'s acceptance — if it waits more than three days, call the owner\'s representative; a disputed report is corrected and resubmitted by the engineer the same week.', 'Work orders pending approval: approve the owner\'s requests or chase yours.', 'Timesheets to approve: approve or reject with a remark; hours that are not approved are not invoiced.', 'My day: your tasks due today and overdue — complete, reschedule or delegate; a task you keep postponing is an issue, write it into the register.', 'Planning: tomorrow\'s assignments of your engineers — are the cars, the spares and the open items in place?', 'Remote sessions: every remote intervention on a covered vessel is logged on its ticket (Log session — date, hours, remark) the same day; the contract\'s hours used must be the truth when the owner asks.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-notifications.jpg', 'cap' => 'The notification page — everything that changed since your last visit'],
      ['fig' => 'g-tasks.jpg', 'cap' => 'My day — tasks due today or overdue'],
      ['tip' => 'Answer in the portal, not by e-mail: an external ticket comment, an approval decision or a note on the offer reaches the right person, is logged and ends up in the vessel history. An e-mail does none of these.'],
      'Need a colleague on something? Type @ in any ticket comment, offer note, weekly note, sub-project comment, approval comment or forecast reply and pick the person: they get "… mentioned you in …" in the bell, which opens exactly that note. Owners can call you the same way, so a mention from the owner\'s side is a reply expected today.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'approvals', 'title' => 'The approvals you own', 'body' => [
      'Five flows need a decision from somebody, and the PM is responsible for keeping each of them moving. Every pending item of your projects is counted in the badges of the menu and listed in the attention list.',
      ['fig' => 'flow-approval.svg', 'cap' => 'Owner approvals — submitted → approved / rejected with comments → next revision'],
      ['ol' => ['Owner approvals (documents): submit from the sub-project › Owner approval tab with document number and revision; the owner approves or rejects with comments on the thread. Rejected: answer the comments on the same thread, upload the next revision, resubmit. Target: a decision within 10 working days — remind the owner through the thread, then through the project meeting.', 'Class submittals: the Class tab lists what went to the society, when, and the due date; record the decision and the certificate or the remarks when they arrive. Overdue class items are red in the weekly report.', 'Work orders: a work order you request from the owner stays Pending approval until the owner approves; a work order the owner requests waits for you — approve it or reject it with the reason, both the same day. Approved work orders are started and finished by the engineer; the service report closes them.', 'Service report acceptance: the engineer submits, the owner accepts or disputes. You do not sign on the owner\'s behalf — you make sure the owner\'s representative has the report, the photos and the open issues, and you chase the acceptance.', 'Timesheets: approve the submitted timesheets of your engineers weekly (Monday reminder); reject with a remark when the project, the sub-project or the hour type is wrong — the engineer corrects and resubmits. Administrators mark approved sheets as invoiced.', 'Offers: the owner approves or rejects an offer from the portal; you set the later statuses (In progress, To be invoiced, Finished) and keep the negotiation in the internal notes of the offer. An offer older than its validity is red in the weekly report — chase it or withdraw it.']],
      ['fig' => 'flow-jo.svg', 'cap' => 'Work orders — draft → pending approval → approved → in progress → done'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-fsr.svg', 'cap' => 'Service reports — draft → submitted → accepted / disputed → re-opened'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-timesheet.svg', 'cap' => 'Timesheets — draft → submitted → approved / rejected → invoiced'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-offer.svg', 'cap' => 'Offers — the statuses and who sets them'],
      ['tip' => 'Never approve a timesheet or a work order "to be done later": the badge on the menu is your to-do list and it should be zero when you leave in the evening.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'tickets', 'title' => 'Tickets — the owner\'s voice', 'body' => [
      'Every request of the owner is a ticket: a fault, a service request, a question, a spare part. Tickets of your projects are your responsibility from the moment they arrive, even before an engineer is assigned.',
      ['fig' => 'flow-ticket.svg', 'cap' => 'The ticket lifecycle'],
      'A ticket that needs a quotation gets its offer from the ticket page: € Offer opens a draft already linked to the ticket, the vessel and the project; the portal numbers it at once from the branch series (GAS-OF-2026-001 / GIT-OF-2026-001; offers imported from the ERP keep their SoftOne number and show an ERP pill); a budgetary offer (AACE class) goes on the main project and is converted to a firm offer on the sub-project once approved; the payment terms of the offer become the proforma slots that Sales issues, and the financial status (offered · invoiced · collected · overdue) shows on the project tree and the sub-project head; add the lines from the rate cards (hours, travelling time, allowances, parts), check the texts, Issue R0 — the PDF is the offer document, the owner approves in the portal, and the offer stays listed on the ticket and the vessel.',
      ['ol' => ['Assign and acknowledge the same day: assign the engineer, set In progress and write one external comment that says what will happen and when. Need a colleague\'s eyes on it? Type @ in the comment and pick the person — they get a notification that opens the comment.', 'Place the ticket under the project / sub-project (and on the vessel, for the tickets imported without one) so it appears in the project history and the weekly report.', 'Work it in the portal: internal comments for the team, external ones for the owner; Waiting for owner when you need an answer; a work order or a service report created from the ticket stays linked.', 'Resolve with a written resolution; close after the owner\'s acceptance. Spare-part tickets show in the weekly report under Open items on parts until they are closed.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-ticket.jpg', 'cap' => 'A ticket for staff — assignment, project placement, status actions, internal / external comments'],
      ['ul' => ['Work › Tickets › Activity shows the response and resolution times per engineer and per customer — the numbers the management reviews monthly.', 'The older FD-nnnn tickets are the history from the previous helpdesk: read-only facts of the vessel, useful before a visit.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'weekly', 'title' => 'The weekly cycle — Thursday update, Friday report', 'body' => [
      'The weekly vessel report (GAS-WR-…) goes to the owner every Friday. It is generated from the portal, so the week\'s work is to have the data right by Thursday evening; Friday morning is for reading it, issuing it and sending it.',
      ['fig' => 'g-weekly-notes.jpg', 'cap' => 'Weekly notes — the progress diary with photos; internal notes stay inside Golden A/S'],
      'Assigning the works: break each sub-project into work packages (Works tab) — what, who, how many hours, from when to when, which deck area — as soon as the budget is set; saving writes the engineers\' planning for you, notifies them, and feeds the weekly works forecast (Plan A areas) and the timesheets (Fill from the plan). Check Work › Workload before you pick people: load per week, first free day, open packages. Thursday: review the packages of next week (dates, people), mark Checked the ones reported Done, re-plan the ones behind.',
      'Sections: the hours you enter per discipline in Budget & actual are the budget of the sections of every stage — Engineering (Electrical, Automation, Mechanical, UI/UX, IT), Production, Field & Installation, HAT / SAT, Commissioning — through the discipline × section matrix in Settings › Stage sections; a discipline feeding several sections gets one cell per section. On the sub-project › Sections tab list the deliverables with their weight in hours and tick them as they are finished — the stages of the process line are fed from the sections automatically. Engineers book their hours on the stage they worked on; whoever needs more hours than the budget cannot book them and sends an extra-hours request with the cause and the reason: management decides (you decide as well for the site stages of your projects) on Work › Engineering requests. The budget stays as agreed with the customer — the approved extra is shown over it (Used % above 100, +N h on Budget & actual, Budget extended indicator) and the percentage is recalculated with it included. A section finished early is declared finished and its remaining budget is released.',
      'Production: a work package that needs equipment built in the shop gets a production order from the Works tab (🏭 + Production order) — the serial numbers, the production manager and the planned dates; from then on Fleet › Equipment › Production shows its phase (design → in production → FAT → delivered → HAT → commissioning → SAT → closed) and the % of every unit as the production manager records them, with the drawings (draft, approved, as-built), FAT / HAT / SAT records, photos, delivery and commissioning documents on the order and on each serial number\'s production card. You are notified when the order moves and when a unit is tested, delivered or accepted. While an order exists, the stages of the sub-project are fed from it — Engineering from the drawings, Procurement from the subcontracting, Production from the serial numbers, FAT / Installation / SAT-HAT / Commissioning from the unit statuses and the records (read-only on the Process tab, 🏭); a stage without evidence keeps your value, and you can Unlock any fed stage to type your own (logged) and Lock it back. Anything built outside — a whole unit or a part — is a Subcontracting line on the order (supplier, PO, due date, incoming inspection); Production › Subcontracting shows every open line and what is late.',
      'Budget vs actual: the navy strip under every sub-project title shows the live result, the hours against budget with the progress tick and the parts against budget; set the budget (hours per discipline, parts) in the Budget & actual tab when the offer is approved — the first revision is pre-filled from the offer lines — and add each purchase order or supplier invoice as a cost entry the day it is placed. Red flags there (hours over budget, parts over budget, hours ahead of progress) are agenda items for Thursday; Projects › € Cost control compares all sub-projects of the project and prints the cost report for the meeting.',
      ['ol' => ['Thursday — progress: open every active sub-project › Process tab, set the stage percentages (a snapshot is kept, the report shows the delta since last week), check the deadline, and tick the invoicing status if it changed.', 'Thursday — weekly notes: one external note per sub-project that had activity (what was done, what is next, photos), internal notes for the things the team must know. No activity is also a note ("Awaiting yard — cabling not ready").', 'Thursday — open issues: open the Open issues tab of every sub-project: close what was solved (with a closing note), start what is in hand, set due dates and assignees on the rest, add what came up in calls and e-mails, pick a category for each. The service reports of the week have already added theirs.', 'Thursday — KPIs and risks: Work › KPIs for your projects — anything red or amber (schedule SPI, cost CPI, scope PSC) is a point for the weekly note; keep the risk log alive (owners, due dates, mark mitigated) — RMI counts what you close.', 'Thursday — registers: drawings received or sent (mark the received ones with ✓ Received on the Drawing tracker and add the new requests — every pending one goes into the report), owner approvals and class submittals decided, tests performed — record them on their tabs; delays (a deadline passed, a stage behind) get a delay note so they appear under Potential delays.', 'Thursday — planning: next week on the Planning board for every engineer of the project, with the open items of the previous reports.', 'Thursday — works forecast: open next week\'s Weekly works forecast (pre-filled from this week), set Plan A areas with sub-projects, works and days, Plan B alternatives, read the owner\'s replies on this week\'s forecast (delays, obstacles, painting) and carry them in; Issue R0 on Friday with the weekly report.', 'Friday — the report: sub-project › Reports tab or Fleet › Vessel › Weekly report: read the PDF preview from the first page to the last (the colour code shows where the attention is), correct the data where needed, then Issue — the revision R0 / R1 … is archived in the project folder and is what the owner downloads.', 'Friday — send and tell: the owner\'s representative gets the link or the PDF; the weekly notes of next week start from the open points of this report.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-weekly.jpg', 'cap' => 'The weekly vessel report page — week navigation, issue, revisions'],
      ['tip' => 'Sending it: after Issue, open the ✉ E-mail card at the foot of the report page — the owner\'s contacts are already in To, the PDF (and Word) are attached, the text is written (from the e-mail templates an administrator keeps in Settings — ask for a change there rather than retyping it every week). Outlook draft gives you an .eml that opens in Outlook as a new message; check it and press Send.'],
      ['fig' => 'g-issues.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Open issues tab of a sub-project — register, filters, close with a note'],
      ['fig' => 'g-issues-weekly.jpg', 'cap' => 'Open issues and closed issues as they appear in the weekly vessel report'],
      ['tip' => 'Read the report as the owner reads it: a red bar in Deadlines, a delay flag or an open issue past its due date will be the first question in the meeting — have the answer in the weekly note before you issue.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'forecast', 'title' => 'The weekly works forecast — Plan A, Plan B and the owner\'s replies', 'body' => [
      'The weekly works forecast (GAS-WF-<project>-<week>) tells the owner, the yard and your own engineers where the team will work next week on the vessel\'s deck plans — and where it will work instead if a space is not available. It is the PM\'s document: one per project and week, drafted on Thursday with the weekly report, issued on Friday, revised when the owner replies. The weekly vessel report carries next week\'s forecast as its last section.',
      ['fig' => 'g-wf-forecast.jpg', 'cap' => 'A forecast — the deck map with Plan A areas solid and Plan B areas dashed'],
      ['ol' => ['Once per vessel: the decks and working areas. Vessel page › Decks & working areas — upload a photo or GA per deck, drag the working areas as rectangles, name and classify them (Interior, Exterior, Machinery, Technical, Guest area, Crew area); areas with no drawing are list entries. Only PMs, managers and administrators edit them.', 'Thursday — open next week: project action row › Weekly works forecast (‹ › moves between weeks). A new week starts as a draft pre-filled from the previous week, so you only change what changes.', 'Plan A: click an area on the deck map (or + Area to Plan A), tick the sub-projects working there — several per area are fine — write the short works description, tick the days. Plan B: Shift-click on the map (or + Area to Plan B) for the alternatives if a Plan A space is blocked by painting, the owner on board or yard works.', 'Carry in the owner\'s replies on this week\'s forecast: the delays, obstacles in spaces and painting he reported are ticked "in the report" by default; untick what should stay out, write the one-line summary of how the schedule was adapted, adjust Plan A / Plan B accordingly.', 'Friday — read the PDF preview (deck maps, Plan A and Plan B tables, the week grid, the owner\'s remarks, notes), then Issue R0: the document is archived under the project, the owner\'s users and the project staff are notified, the forecast is locked.', 'When the owner replies after the issue (his reply arrives in your bell): Re-open, adapt, Issue again — R1, R2 …; the owner always sees the last issued revision and every revision stays in the archive.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-wf-editor.jpg', 'cap' => 'The draft editor — plan, area, sub-projects, works and days per row'],
      ['fig' => 'g-wf-replies.jpg', 'cap' => 'The owner\'s replies — change requests, delays, obstacles, painting; ticked ones go into the final schedule'],
      ['ul' => ['Use the forecast in the project meeting: the deck maps are the picture to agree access, painting and crew movements with the owner and the yard.', 'Before a visit the engineer reads the issued forecast: Plan A areas to go to, Plan B alternatives if a space is blocked.', 'A forecast without Plan B is a promise you cannot keep when a space is closed — give every Plan A area at least one alternative.', 'Administrators set the opacity of the deck photos (Settings › Vessel systems › Deck photos) so the rectangles stand out on busy photographs.']],
      ['tip' => 'Draft the forecast right after the stages and the weekly notes on Thursday: the areas follow from what the sub-projects will do next week, and the owner gets Friday\'s weekly report and the forecast together.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'meeting', 'title' => 'Preparing the project meeting', 'body' => [
      'A project meeting with the owner or the yard is prepared from the portal in half an hour, because every list you need is already there. Prepare in this order and print or issue what you will show.',
      ['ol' => ['Agenda = the weekly report: issue the current week\'s report (or the vessel history for a kick-off / review meeting) — it is the agenda in the owner\'s order: progress, notes, deadlines, delays, open issues, parts, offers, approvals, class, work orders, service reports, tickets.', 'Next week\'s working areas: open next week\'s Weekly works forecast — the deck maps with Plan A and Plan B are the picture to agree with the owner and the yard (access, painting, crew on board); the owner\'s replies on this week\'s forecast are the first items to settle.', 'Open issues, sorted: on each sub-project › Open issues tab filter Open and sort by Due date; the overdue ones (red) and the ones due this week (amber) are discussed first; export the list to Excel if the owner keeps his own list — the numbers OI-… make the two lists comparable.', 'Decisions you need: owner approvals pending and class submittals without an answer — prepare the question per document; offers pending — amount, validity, what blocks the decision (from the internal notes).', 'Work ahead: next two weeks on the Planning board for the project\'s engineers, the work orders waiting for approval, the mobilisations that need the yard\'s access or the owner\'s spares.', 'The owner\'s items: tickets Waiting for owner, disputed service reports, drawings expected from the owner or the yard that are overdue (Drawings tab).', 'Costs when relevant: Man-hours per project or sub-project for the period, and the invoicing status of each sub-project (financial roles only).', 'During the meeting: take the minutes directly as open issues (one per decision or action, with assignee, due date and the side that acts — Golden A/S, owner / crew, third party) and as tasks for your team. The minutes are then already in next week\'s report.', 'After the meeting: a weekly note "Project meeting dd.mm — decisions" per sub-project concerned, issue a revision of the weekly report if the owner asked for corrections, and update the planning with the agreed dates.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-reports-tab.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Reports tab of a sub-project — Issue button and the issued revisions'],
      ['fig' => 'g-planning.jpg', 'cap' => 'The planning board — the next two weeks of the project\'s engineers'],
      ['tip' => 'Open the portal on the screen during the meeting instead of a slide deck: filtering the open issues live and closing an item in front of the owner is worth more than any presentation — and the minutes write themselves.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'monthly', 'title' => 'The monthly closing — first working days of the month', 'body' => [
      'On the 1st the portal reminds you of the monthly sub-project report, the vessel history and the timesheet statement. The monthly closing is also the moment to look at the project as a whole — not only at this week.',
      ['ol' => ['Monthly sub-project report (GAS-PR-…): one per active sub-project from the Reports tab — stages, drawings, owner approvals, class, tests, open issues of the last 30 days. Read, correct the registers, Issue.', 'Vessel technical history (GAS-VH-…): issue it on the vessel page when something was accepted or closed this month; it is the dossier the owner keeps.', 'Accounting report: Commercial › Accounting › New — the month\'s approved hours of your project (timesheets and service reports) collected, priced from the rate cards, reviewed line by line (include / exclude, discounts), issued as GAS-AR-… and e-mailed to accounting; accounting answers in the portal and the timesheets and service reports turn Invoiced by themselves. Hours already reported are never offered again.', 'Hours: all timesheets of the month approved; Man-hours per project checked against the budget; the timesheet statement (GAS-TS-…) issued for the projects that are invoiced on hours; administrators mark the invoiced sheets.', 'Invoicing status: update partial / full and the invoiced percentage per sub-project; a finished sub-project without full invoicing is a note for the management.', 'Offers: Commercial › Offers filtered on your projects — aging, validity, status; withdraw or renew what is older than its validity, set To be invoiced / Finished for what was delivered.', 'Equipment: the service history of the units of the vessel shows the month\'s visits; register serial numbers that appeared in the service reports as NO-SN entries with the correct number.', 'Tickets: Work › Tickets › Activity for the month — response and resolution times per engineer of your projects; tickets older than 30 days are closed or turned into an issue with a due date.', 'Remote support contracts: Library › IT & Cybersecurity filtered Expiring soon — offer the renewal before the notice period runs out (the renewal task is in your list); contracts over 80 % of their hours get a word to the owner before the hours run out.', 'Completed work: set the completed sub-projects to Completed (they drop out of the engineers\' forms and the planning), and the project to Completed when the last one is; keep the files in the archive.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-manhours.jpg', 'cap' => 'Man-hours per project with the report buttons and the issued statements'],
      ['fig' => 'g-offers.jpg', 'cap' => 'The offer list — aging and status per project'],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-vessel.jpg', 'cap' => 'The equipment of a vessel with the records of the month'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'fieldwork', 'title' => 'Field work — before, during and after a visit', 'body' => [
      'Every visit of an engineer produces a service report (SF-GLD-014) or an inspection report (SF-GLD-020). The PM prepares the visit, checks the report before the owner sees it and takes the open issues into the register.',
      ['ol' => ['Before: check the issued weekly works forecast — the engineer goes to the Plan A areas and knows the Plan B alternatives if a space is blocked; plan the visit on the Planning board (the editor lists the open items of the previous reports), make sure the work order is approved when the owner requires one, the spares are ordered (tickets on parts), the contacts on board are on the project Contacts tab.', 'During: the engineer keeps the report as a draft on the vessel — tasks with start / end and type, parts, photos per task, equipment serviced; you can read the draft and comment internally.', 'After: the engineer submits; the PDF and the Word document are archived and the owner is notified. Read the submitted report: wrong sub-project, missing photos, an open issue without a clear next step — ask the engineer to re-open and resubmit (R1) before the owner disputes it.', 'Open issues: the open issues of the report are now entries of the register with the report as source — give them a category, an assignee and a due date; the engineer\'s wording stays.', 'Inspection reports: the risk level per system (low / medium / high / critical) is the headline; a high or critical system becomes an issue, an offer or a work order within the week.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-fsr.jpg', 'cap' => 'A submitted and accepted service report'],
      ['fig' => 'g-isr.jpg', 'cap' => 'An issued inspection report — inspected systems with their risk levels'],
      ['fig' => 'g-planning-editor.jpg', 'cap' => 'The assignment editor — range, sub-project, car, note and the open items of the previous reports'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'closeout', 'title' => 'Project close-out', 'body' => [
      'A project is closed when every sub-project is completed, every approval decided, every issue closed or handed over, every timesheet approved and invoiced and the final documents issued.',
      ['ol' => ['Open issues: close what is done with a closing note; what remains for the owner or a third party is handed over in the last weekly report and closed with "handed over — see WR …".', 'Final revisions: last weekly report, monthly report, vessel history R-n, timesheet statement; the as-built drawings and the certificates in the Files tab with the right category and visibility.', 'Equipment: serial numbers, warranty dates and the contacts of every unit correct — this is what the service engineers will find in two years.', 'Statuses: sub-projects Completed, project Completed; offers Finished; tickets closed.', 'Lessons: an internal weekly note "Close-out — lessons learned" on the main sub-project, so the next PM of this owner finds it in the history.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-archive.jpg', 'cap' => 'The project archive — issued revisions and files'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'reading', 'title' => 'Reading the reports — the colour code', 'body' => [
      'Every generated document (weekly report, vessel history, monthly report, inspection report, equipment history) uses the same colour code, explained in a legend under its title: a narrow coloured bar at the left of a row that needs attention and the causing figure in the same colour. Red — overdue, failed, high or critical risk, rejected, delayed, an offer past its validity; amber — due within 7 days, pending, medium risk, draft; green — done, on time, accepted, closed, progress gained; navy — in progress; grey — not applicable. A report without bars is a project without open attention points.',
      ['fig' => 'g-colour-code.jpg', 'cap' => 'The weekly vessel report with the legend and the row markers'],
      ['ul' => ['The lists in the portal sort by any column (click the header) and remember your filters — set "Open, sorted by due date" once on the Open issues tab and it stays; an amber banner says when a remembered selection is in force, with Clear in it.', 'The Projects page shows the open tickets of every project (badge on the project line, Open tickets block under the offers and tasks) — the quickest daily check per project.', 'The document numbers: GAS-WR (weekly), GAS-PR (monthly), GAS-VH (vessel history), GAS-TS (timesheet statement), GAS-EH (equipment history), GAS-WF (weekly works forecast), GAS-RS (remote support contract), OI-<project>-nnn (open issue), <project>-JO-nnnn (work order), yyyymmdd + initials (service report), ISR-… (inspection report), TK-… / FD-… (tickets).']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'checklists', 'title' => 'Checklists', 'body' => [
      'The routine on one page. Print this section and keep it next to the screen for the first month.',
      ['ul' => ['DAILY (morning, 15 min): bell · packages marked Done by engineers checked · new / unassigned tickets assigned and acknowledged · tickets waiting for owner > 7 days nudged · submitted service reports chased (> 3 days) · work orders pending decided · timesheets approved · remote sessions of the day logged on their tickets · My day tasks done or rescheduled · tomorrow\'s planning checked.']],
      ['ul' => ['WEEKLY (Thursday): stages updated on every active sub-project · work packages of next week assigned (Works tab), Done packages checked, Workload reviewed · cost entries of the week added, budget flags reviewed (Budget & actual) · one weekly note per sub-project (external) · open issues closed / started / dated / categorised · drawings, approvals, class, tests recorded · delays flagged · next week planned · works forecast drafted (Plan A / Plan B, owner replies carried in). (Friday): weekly report read page by page · corrected · issued R-n · e-mailed to the owner\'s contacts from the ✉ E-mail card (PDF attached) · works forecast issued and e-mailed the same way.']],
      ['ul' => ['PROJECT MEETING: weekly report issued (agenda) and e-mailed from the ✉ E-mail card · open issues filtered Open and sorted by due date, exported if needed · approvals and class pending with the question per document · offers pending with the blocker · next two weeks planning · owner\'s items (tickets waiting, disputed reports, overdue drawings) · minutes typed as issues and tasks during the meeting · weekly note "Project meeting — decisions" after.']],
      ['ul' => ['MONTHLY (1st–3rd): monthly sub-project report issued per active sub-project · vessel history issued when something changed · all timesheets approved, man-hours checked, statement issued · invoicing status updated · offers aging reviewed · remote support contracts expiring or over 80 % of hours handled · equipment records and serial numbers completed · ticket activity reviewed, old tickets closed or converted · completed sub-projects / projects set Completed.']],
      ['ul' => ['CLOSE-OUT: issues closed or handed over · final revisions issued · as-built files and certificates filed · equipment data complete · statuses Completed / Finished · lessons-learned note.']],
